--TEST--
`Core\Script\ExitReport::memoryPeak` is the request's high-water mark read at the ending, so it shows a spike the script already gave back, and memory a hook uses does not move it — `rule:observability/script-on-exit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Script\ExitReport::memoryPeak
// The spike is a 4 MiB string, released before the script ends. A reading of
// the held figure at the ending would no longer see it, and the peak still does.
// The hook then holds 8 MiB of its own: the report was built before the hook
// ran, so a second reading is the same number.
Core\Script::onExit(fn (Core\Script\ExitReport $report): void => {
    int $peak = $report->memoryPeak();
    if ($peak >= 4194304) {
        echo "peak: the spike is recorded\n";
    } else {
        echo "peak: the spike is missing\n";
    }

    string $inHook = Core\Str::repeat("y", 8388608);
    if ($report->memoryPeak() == $peak) {
        echo "peak: fixed at the ending\n";
    } else {
        echo "peak: moved by the hook\n";
    }
    echo "hook held ", Core\Str::length($inHook), " bytes\n";
});

string $spike = Core\Str::repeat("x", 4194304);
echo "spike held ", Core\Str::length($spike), " bytes\n";
$spike = "";
echo "the last statement ran\n";
--EXPECT--
spike held 4194304 bytes
the last statement ran
peak: the spike is recorded
peak: fixed at the ending
hook held 8388608 bytes
